WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:08.750
We introduce a new data
structure, the bilateral grid,

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:12.280
which enables real-time,
edge-aware image processing.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.550
2D images are lifted
onto a coarse, 3D grid,

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:18.670
where the z-component
corresponds to intensity.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.190
As a result, points
across a strong edge

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:22.750
are distant in the grid.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.090
We introduce slicing,
an operation that

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:26.980
exploits this
property to extract

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:31.090
a discontinuous, 2D image
from a smooth bilateral grid.

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:33.670
We demonstrate a variety
of real-time, edge-aware

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:36.340
algorithms using
the bilateral grid.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.070
We demonstrate real-time,
bilateral filtering

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:41.770
on a noisy, 12-megapixel input.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:44.170
The user can explore
the parameter's base

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:47.440
while getting real-time
feedback on the whole image.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.920
We zoom in to show the full
resolution of the image.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.380
Real-time feedback makes
it easy for the user

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:56.380
to fine-tune filter parameters.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:59.350
We further accelerate the
bilateral filter on video

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:02.890
by sampling a random
10% of the input pixels.

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:06.580
Sub-sampling causes swimming
artifacts, shown on the left.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:08.650
We eliminate these
artifacts by applying

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:12.690
a temporal exponential
filter, shown on the right.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.390
We demonstrate the real-time
video abstraction technique

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.060
by [INAUDIBLE] and
colleagues on HD video.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.700
We adapt real-time
video abstraction

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.070
to follow a point of
focus, here controlled

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:26.600
by the mouse pointer.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:29.120
Elements that are farther
away from the point of focus

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.680
are more abstracted.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.440
We first compute the distance
to the point of focus

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.290
and cross bilateral filter
it with the input image

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:39.440
to create an adapted
importance map that respects

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.000
the edges of the image.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.810
We use this map to composite the
levels of a bilateral pyramid.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:47.930
The rest of the abstraction
pipeline remains unchanged.

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:49.880
Note that our method
is efficient enough

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.820
to compute five bilateral
filters per frame

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.505
and allow parameter adjustment
while the video plays.

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:01.510
We transfer the look
of a model photograph

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:04.490
to an input video in real time.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.820
Our result captures the
tonal balance and level

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:09.490
of detail of the model
in the spirit of the work

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.110
by Bay and colleagues.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.990
Our method enables on-the-fly
adjustment of parameters

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:15.670
on live, HD video.

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:17.290
Compared to existing
techniques that

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:19.720
require offline
processing, our approach

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.060
makes it easy to modify
the level of detail

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.670
and adjust the overall
tonal balance of this shot

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:26.110
as it plays.

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:28.240
We demonstrate
bilateral grid painting

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:31.060
by locally modifying
the hue while respecting

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.280
strong intensity edges.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:35.230
The initial mouse
click locks the brush

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:37.080
to an intensity level.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.220
The brush is aware
of edges and does not

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:42.340
paint across intensity
discontinuities.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:45.130
For instance, the brush does
not affect the white wall

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:47.970
by the door.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:50.700
Our method runs in
real time on the GPU.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.120
We update the entire 2-megapixel
input on every frame.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:06.150
We apply the same
technique to locally modify

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.980
tone-mapping parameters.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:14.910
We adjust exposure parameters on
this 15-megapixel HDR panorama.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:18.420
We paint over the gate to
correct the overexposed region.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.430
Notice how the bars
are unaffected.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.540
Similarly, the
windows are unaffected

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.510
while the overexposed
dome is corrected.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:43.410
Our GPU algorithm updates
the entire 15-megapixel image

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:44.310
on every frame.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.630
We demonstrate
scribble interpolation

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:52.940
using the bilateral grid.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:55.580
We scribble on the input
image, shown on the left.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:01.020
The extracted influence
map is shown on the right.

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:11.730
We use the influence map
to drive a color shift.

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:13.380
The white scribbles
cause the cloth

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:16.200
to change from red to blue,
while the black scribbles

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.610
protect the candies.

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:21.620
We zoom in to show the
full image resolution.

